### Using a Statefulset instead of Deployment

If you're deploying this with Kubernetes in a statefulset mode, you'll need to make sure that the database pods are started before starting your webapp pod(s). If not, you may get errors similar to the following:

```
Error: Post http://<pod-name>.db:<port>/status: dial tcp <ip>:443: connect: connection refused
```

To fix this, you can add an initContainer to start the database (and wait until it has fully booted):

```yaml
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata:
  name: db
  labels:
    app: db
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: db
  serviceName: db
  replicas: 1
  updateStrategy:
    type: RollingUpdate
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: db
    spec:
      containers:
        # ...
      initContainers:
        - name: wait-for-db
          image: alpine:latest
          command: ['sh', '-c']
          args:
            - while ! nc -z db 5432; do echo waiting for postgres ... sleep 2; done; exit 0
          volumeMounts:
            - mountPath: /tmp/wait-test
              name: wait-test-volume
      volumes:
        - name: wait-test-volume
          emptyDir: {}
```

### Adding Custom Headers

We recommend adding custom headers to your ingress rules so that you can easily verify that they were successfully applied. For example, you could add this to your configmap:

```yaml
  nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/custom-request-header: X-Custom-Header: foo
```

You can then verify that the header was added by running `curl -I http://some-host.example.com`. You should see something like the below output.

```shell
$ curl -I http://my-app.example.com
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Server: nginx/1.21.7
Date: Mon, 26 Apr 2023 03:44:26 GMT
Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8
Transfer-Encoding: chunked
Connection: keep-alive
X-Powered-By: Express
Via: 1.1 vegur
Custom-Request-Header: X-Custom-Header: foo
```

